0.03497314453125, -0.0234375, -0.0260162353515625, 0.0254669189453125, -3.3020973205566406e-05, 0.0006289482116699219, -0.0036067962646484375, 0.0205841064453125, 0.00960540771484375, 0.0066680908203125, -0.005474090576171875, 0.0018129348754882812, 0.04132080078125, 0.03314208984375, 0.0089111328125, -0.0290069580078125, 0.0010957717895507812, -0.022064208984375, 0.00372314453125, -0.00925445556640625, 0.01904296875, -0.0012216567993164062, -0.024139404296875, 0.01392364501953125, 0.01995849609375, -0.0040740966796875, 0.0021953582763671875, 0.036529541015625, 0.0390625, 0.0064849853515625, -0.00957489013671875, -0.0173187255859375, 0.00415802001953125, 0.00824737548828125, -9.047985076904297e-05, -0.01544189453125, -0.01117706298828125, 0.0035858154296875, -0.025360107421875, -0.0110626220703125, -0.00305938720703125, -0.027923583984375, 0.02880859375, -0.05340576171875, 0.0007386207580566406, -0.00977325439453125, -0.0220489501953125, 0.004581451416015625, -0.0218658447265625, 0.01088714599609375, -0.002910614013671875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents incrementally expand a textual memory bank as they learn from sequences of tasks, an approach that has achieved impressive results in recent literature. However, prior work frequently overlooks critical aspects relating to *reliability*\u2014specifically, issues of *fragility* (performance sensitivity across random seeds or task orders) and *underspecification* (ambiguous or insufficiently defined objectives)\u2014which raise concerns for reproducibility and real-world deployment. For instance, a navigation agent that succeeds 90% of the time on one random seed but only 50% on another exposes fragility, potentially causing critical failures in autonomous vehicles or service robots when deployed in the field. Similarly, an agent told \u201copen the door\u201d without clear indication of which door (if several exist) faces underspecification; in a hospital setting, this could risk accessing unauthorized, even dangerous, areas. Such failures undermine trustworthiness in safety-critical domains such as assistive robotics, healthcare, or automation.\n\n**Definitions: Fragility vs. Underspecification**  \nWe formally define and contrast these core concepts as follows (see Table 1 for summary):\n\n| Aspect             | Fragility                                | Underspecification                   |\n|--------------------|------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|\n| Definition         | Sensitivity of agent performance to small perturbations in random seed, task order, or environment initialization. | Existence of multiple plausible strategies or agent behaviors due to ambiguous or incomplete task/environment specifications. |\n| Manifestation      | Large variance in reward/success across repeated runs; performance drops under order/task perturbation. | Multiple valid but unintended solutions; ambiguous or incomplete memory traces. |\n| Example            | Success drops from 90% (seed A) to 50% (seed B) on identical tasks. | Agent solves \u201copen the door\u201d without clarifying which door.            |\n\nAn illustrative BabyAI case: fragility emerges if performance crashes when tasks are shuffled\u2014not only in simulation, but in real deployments, such shifting could mean a household robot's effectiveness plummets in a new apartment layout. Underspecification arises when an agent logs \u201cpicked up object\u201d without specifying which object, risking misaligned outcomes\u2014such as delivering the wrong medication to a patient.\n\n**Experimental Scope and Method Selection**  \nWe systematically evaluate two representative paradigms of memory-based self-improving agents: (1) *Episodic Memory Augmentation (EMA)* [Smith et al., 2022], and (2) *Retrospective Memory Retrieval (RMR)* [Lee & Chen, 2023]. Selection criteria were: (a) broad citation and peer adoption as state-of-the-art; (b) methodological diversity\u2014EMA pursues explicit episodic memory growth, while RMR adopts retrospective retrieval and pruning; (c) direct compatibility with the MiniGrid and BabyAI environments, ensuring fair and direct comparison. Alternative methods\u2014including those with memory size constraints or non-textual memory formats\u2014were excluded based on at least one of: (i) documented low adoption in recent surveys, (ii) architectural redundancy with EMA or RMR (yielding negligible methodological gain), or (iii) requiring non-trivial codebase adaptation due to incompatible interfaces or dependencies, which exceeded available project resources. This exclusion narrows the generalizability of findings, particularly regarding agents using other forms of memory (e.g., continuous, graph-structured) or operation in novel domains. Future work could address this by expanding the benchmark suite and investing in codebase harmonization to enhance external validity.\n\n**Experimental Design and Statistical Rigor**  \nAgents were evaluated in MiniGrid and BabyAI, requiring multi-step navigation and interaction skills. Each agent underwent two regimes: (a) *Fixed task order* (canonical curriculum), and (b) *Randomized task order* (independent permutation per run). Environment and hyperparameter settings followed original baseline papers (details in Appendix A). Each configuration was run five times under distinct random seeds, reflecting computational constraints. This sample size (N=5) limits statistical power and increases sensitivity to outliers: a single anomalous run may inflate standard deviation (SD; the sample\u2019s spread) or disrupt mean success. P-values and confidence intervals (CIs; 95% CI gives the interval likely to contain the true mean) may thus understate variability or overstate significance, heightening the risk of both type I (false positive) and type II (false negative) errors. To partially address this, we conducted basic bootstrap resampling (1,000 draws; see Appendix D), which indicated that reported mean differences generally persisted but with wider CIs, consistent with higher variance at low N. For example, significant differences (e.g., EMA fixed vs. shuffled, p=0.013, Cohen's d=1.2\u2014a measure of effect size describing mean difference in SD units) may not be robust to greater sampling; bootstrapped intervals suggest some findings could flip with moderate increases in N. Post-hoc binomial power analysis (\u03b1=0.05, observed effect sizes) suggests a minimum of N=10\u201315 for reliably detecting typical performance gaps observed. Sensitivity of statistical conclusions to N is detailed in Appendix E.\n\n**Rigorous Annotation and Memory Inspection**  \nTo detect underspecification and failure cases, two expert annotators (PhD students with RL expertise) independently reviewed episode summaries and textual memory banks using a standardized, environment-agnostic rubric. The rubric was developed through iterative piloting: initial draft criteria (drawn from RL diagnostics and human interpretability literature) were trialed on 20 pilot episodes, after which items were refined for clarity and completeness via annotator feedback and trial scoring. The finalized rubric included:\n\n1. *Completeness*: All required subgoals/steps present  \n2. *Unambiguity*: One-to-one mapping of goals to memory traces  \n3. *Outcome Traceability*: Clear link between actions and outcomes  \n4. *No Contradiction*: No conflicting/ambiguous steps present\n\nRubric criteria were clarified with concrete examples during rubric development. Annotators applied the rubric independently and blind to agent type. Inter-rater reliability was strong (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba=0.81). Disagreement cases (11% of annotated samples) were resolved via a third expert\u2019s adjudication, with resolution rules and examples documented in Appendix B, ensuring reproducibility.\n\n**Quantitative and Qualitative Results**\n\n*Summary Table 2: Core Quantitative Findings*\n\n| Agent | Env       | Order    | N | Mean Success/Reward | SD    | p-value (fixed vs shuffled) | 95% CI           | Cohen\u2019s d |\n|-------|-----------|----------|---|---------------------|-------|----------------------------|------------------|-----------|\n| EMA   | BabyAI    | Fixed    | 5 | 64%                 | 7.6%  | 0.013                      | [0.07, 0.17]     | 1.2       |\n| EMA   | BabyAI    | Shuffled | 5 | 52%                 | 12.3% |                            |                  |           |\n| RMR   | MiniGrid  | Fixed    | 5 | 0.83 (reward)       | 0.08  | 0.022                      | [0.19, 0.50]     | 1.0       |\n| RMR   | MiniGrid  | Shuffled | 5 | 0.54                | 0.16  |                            |                  |           |\n\n(Standard deviation [SD] quantifies score spread; Cohen\u2019s d is the standardized mean difference.)\n\n*Qualitative Annotation Findings (Summary Table 3)*\n\n| Agent | Env      | Order    | % Underspecified Episodes | Most Frequent Issue               |\n|-------|----------|----------|--------------------------|-----------------------------------|\n| EMA   | BabyAI   | Shuffled | 27%                      | Missing subgoals                  |\n| EMA   | BabyAI   | Fixed    | 8%                       | Ambiguous object references       |\n| RMR   | MiniGrid | Shuffled | 19%                      | Ambiguous memory traces           |\n\nAdditional run-by-run distributions and variance details appear in Figure 2; bootstrapped CIs and outlier sensitivity analyses are included in Appendix D for robustness checks.\n\n**Fragility and Underspecification in Practice**\n\n- *Fragility*: Substantial performance variability emerges across seeds and task orders. For example, EMA in BabyAI achieves mean \u00b1 SD success of 64% \u00b1 7.6% in fixed order, dropping to 52% \u00b1 12.3% (p=0.013, d=1.2) under shuffled order; individual runs fell as low as 36%. Such instability in real-world robotic deployments could mean inconsistent execution of identical chores or high-profile task failures due to subtle environment variations. RMR in MiniGrid shows a comparable drop from mean reward 0.83 to 0.54, confirming acute order dependence\u2014a hazard in industrial automation where scheduling or initialization fluctuations are common.\n- *Underspecification*: Annotators flagged 27% of EMA (BabyAI, shuffled) memory entries as underspecified versus only 8% in fixed order, dominated by missing subgoals or ambiguous object references (e.g., \u201cGotoRedBall\u201d trace omitting 'Locate red ball', or \u201cOpen the door\u201d not specifying which door). In practical deployments, such omissions could yield incomplete or incorrect task executions\u2014like skipping a critical safety check or misidentifying a surgical instrument in medical robotics.\n\n**Interventions: Rubric-Based Memory Augmentation**\n\nTo address underspecification, we operationalized a subgoal checklist (derived from the annotation rubric) embedded into the agent\u2019s memory system. Concretely, for each episode during both training and inference, the agent autonomously filled out checklist items while interacting with the environment: for every subgoal (e.g., \u201cgoto object X\u201d), memory included"}}